    <div class="research-intro">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Systems in development</p>
        <h2 id="research-title">ForgeWire organizes the work. Fabric explores remote execution.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>They address different infrastructure questions. The public overview describes ForgeWire as an application and runtime around intelligent work, and Fabric as a separate control-plane direction for policy-gated work across machines.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="research-projects">
      <article class="research-project" data-project-id="forgewire">
        <div class="research-project-heading"><h3><a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview">ForgeWire <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><span class="status status--private">Private development</span></div>
        <p>ForgeWire is being built around the work: coordinating configured models, tools, project context, and selected workflows toward outcomes a person can review. Some run paths and capability contracts remain in development.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview">Read the public overview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
      <article class="research-project" data-project-id="fabric">
        <div class="research-project-heading"><h3><a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview/blob/85d1905b0bd8db420882f00065daff34833dab27/README.md#3-fabric-dispatch-and-remote-execution">Fabric <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><span class="status status--private">Private development</span></div>
        <p>Fabric is a separate execution control-plane direction for authenticated, policy-gated work across machines. The public overview describes a limited, configuration-dependent connection for selected ForgeWire workflows; Fabric is not a public service.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

